
{"id":"6068fe62-f945-41f4-a8cc-8eadaf908556","object":"chat.completion","created":1785013102,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        children_map[nid] = []\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children_map[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required:\\n            continue\\n        children = children_map[nid]\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children):\\n            continue\\n        # Check that all required symbols still present after removal (redundant given above, but safe)\\n        counts = {}\\n        for rid in retained:\\n            s = node_map[rid]['symbol']\\n            if s in required:\\n                counts[s] = counts.get(s, 0) + 1\\n        # n's symbol is not required, so no change needed\\n        # remove node\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for SE-PE-01. The task is about grammar-guided node reduction on a tree structure. The payload contains nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list.\n\nGoal: remove nodes while preserving required symbols and grammar validity? The description mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\", \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\", but we don't have grammar or parser. Likely the tree is a parse tree where children must be in a certain order? However the fixture payload doesn't provide grammar rules, only nodes with parent->child relationships. The required_symbols list indicates which symbols must remain present in the tree after removal.\n\nThe algorithm: treat nodes as tree. We must decide which removable nodes can be removed. The objective: reduce the tree by removing removable nodes, but we must ensure that for each required symbol, at least one node with that symbol remains. Also we must ensure that after removal, the tree structure remains valid? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion\", but without grammar, maybe the condition is simply that you cannot remove a node if it would cause a required symbol to be missing, or if it has retained children? Actually: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That's a constraint.\n\nAlso: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So removal is allowed only if all required symbols are still present somewhere in the tree after removal.\n\nThe order: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process nodes in descending depth order. At equal depth, use node ID as tie break? It says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we sort all removable nodes by depth descending, then by node id ascending? Or descending? Usually tie break is for ordering the removal candidates. \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" Means when two nodes have same depth, we use their ID to decide order. Probably alphabetical/numeric order. The node IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. Lexicographic order works.\n\nThen we iterate through candidates, for each one, if it is removable and all required symbols would still be present after removal, we remove it. But also we must ensure that a node with retained children cannot be removed? The rule \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That means before removing a node, we need to check if any of its children have been retained (i.e., not removed). Since we are removing nodes, we need to track which nodes are already removed. Initially all nodes are present. As we remove nodes, they are gone. If node has children that are still present (retained), we cannot remove the node because that would orphan those children? Or would children be removed along with parent? In tree reduction, if you remove a parent, its children are also removed because the tree structure collapses? In Perses, removing a node means removing the whole subtree? The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This implies that removing a node removes it and its entire subtree? Because required symbols might be in descendants. But careful: If we remove a node, its descendants are gone. So we must check that all required symbols are present somewhere else (not necessarily in that subtree). The rule \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" suggests that we are not removing the children when we remove the node; rather, we are considering deletion of the node and promoting its children? Or deleting just the node and replacing it with its children? The problem statement: \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" - maybe we can replace a node with its children (if they are in correct order) or delete a leaf? However the fixture shows only nodes with parent references, no mention of siblings order or replacement.\n\nMaybe the intended interpretation is simpler: We have a tree. We want to remove as many removable nodes as possible while preserving that for each required symbol, at least one node with that symbol remains. We can remove any removable node as long as it doesn't break that condition. But we also must not remove nodes that have children that are still present? That seems contradictory: If we remove a node, its children are no longer present because they become disconnected? But the condition \"node with retained children\" might mean that if a node has children that we intend to keep (i.e., not remove), then we cannot remove the parent because that would leave children without a parent? But children can survive without parent? Actually in a tree, if you remove a node, its children become disconnected from the root, so effectively they are also removed from the tree. So if you want to keep children, you must keep the parent. So rule: can't remove a node if any of its descendants are still retained (i.e., not removed). But that's equivalent to: you can only remove a node if all nodes in its subtree (including itself) have been removed or are removable and you remove them? That's not efficient.\n\nAlternatively, the phrase \"retained children\" might refer to children that are not removed? So if a node has children that are not removed, you cannot remove the node because you'd leave those children hanging. So the reduction algorithm goes bottom-up: you can only remove a node after all its children have been removed (or if it has no children). That aligns with \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process deeper nodes first. We remove leaf nodes that are removable and satisfy required symbols condition. After a leaf is removed, its parent might become a leaf? But the parent's children are gone, so then the parent could be removable if all required symbols still exist. That seems like a typical tree reduction where you delete subtrees.\n\nGiven that we have depth attribute and we are told to consider deeper nodes first, that strongly suggests a post-order traversal: process children before parents. So we need to decide for each node whether it can be removed, but only after processing its children.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Build tree from nodes.\n- Identify root (parent null).\n- Perform DFS post-order traversal. For each node (starting from leaves), we evaluate if it can be removed:\n   - Must be removable == True.\n   - Must not be required symbol (i.e., node.symbol not in required_symbols? Actually rule: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So cannot remove if node.symbol is in required_symbols? But \"required symbol\" means the symbol itself must be present, not necessarily that every node with that symbol must be kept. So we can remove a node that has a required symbol if there is another node with that symbol elsewhere. However the rule says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" - that might be interpreted as never remove a node whose symbol is required. That is a hard constraint. So we cannot remove any node that has a symbol that is in required_symbols. That's what the fixture shows: root (symbol root) is required, n1 (symbol s1) is required in all cases. n1 is not removable. So indeed, removable nodes have symbols like s2, s0, etc. But in case a-000, n2 (symbol s2) is removable, n4 (symbol s1) is removable but s1 is required. That would be a conflict: n4 has symbol s1 (required) but is removable=True. According to the rule, we should not remove a required symbol. So maybe the rule means we cannot remove a node if that node's symbol is required, regardless of other nodes with same symbol. So n4 should not be removed. Let's check payload: in a-000, required_symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"]. n4 has symbol \"s1\" and removable true. If we cannot remove required symbols, then n4 is not removable. That aligns with the rule. So we should not remove any node whose symbol is in required_symbols.\n\n- Also, must have no retained children. In post-order, we would have already decided which children are removed. If any child is still present (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove this node because children remain. So we need to track which nodes are retained (not removed). At the end, retained nodes are those not removed.\n\n- Additionally, before removing a node, we must check that all required symbols are still represented in the remaining tree. Since we are only considering removal of one node at a time, we need to ensure that after removal, at least one node of each required symbol remains. This can be checked by counting occurrences of each required symbol in the current tree (nodes not yet removed), and ensuring that after removal, each symbol count >0. If removing this node would cause a required symbol to disappear (i.e., that node is the only one with that symbol), then skip removal.\n\n- The order of processing: deeper nodes first. But we must also consider tie-breaking by node ID. So we need to sort all nodes by depth descending, then by node ID (ascending). Then iterate in that order. But note: if we process parent after children, that's automatically achieved if we sort by depth descending and then process each node, but we must ensure that we only remove a node if its children have been processed (either removed or decided to keep). By sorting by depth descending, children (higher depth) come before parents (lower depth) because depth increases downward. So that's correct.\n\n- When processing a node, we first check if it is removable and symbol not required. Then check if any of its children are still retained (i.e., not removed). If any child retained, skip (cannot remove). Then check required symbols count: count occurrences of each required symbol among currently retained nodes. If removing this node would reduce count for any required symbol to zero, skip removal. Otherwise, remove the node: mark it as removed, add to removed_node_ids list.\n\n- At the end, collect retained_node_ids (IDs of nodes not removed). Return sorted lists? The output requires \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\" as arrays of strings. The schema doesn't specify sorting, but the description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (maybe lexicographically), and removed_node_ids should be in the order of removal (the order we removed them). So we maintain a list removed = [] and append node.id when we remove.\n\n- The tree might have siblings; no need to consider order.\n\n- Also need to consider that the root node (depth 0, symbol root) is not removable (removable false) and is required, so it stays.\n\n- Edge cases: nodes with no children (leaves). Those are processed first.\n\n- Ensure we handle the case where a node is removable but has children that are also removable; we remove children first, then parent becomes eligible if all children gone.\n\n- The required symbols check should be done after conceptually removing the node, but before actual removal. So we need temporary counts. We can precompute a map of symbol->count from the current retained set, then for the node being considered, decrement its symbol count and check if any becomes 0. If not, proceed.\n\n- Implementation: parse case[\"nodes\"] into list of dicts. Build a dict id->node. Also build a dict parent_id->list of children ids for easy child retrieval. Also compute initial symbol counts. We'll maintain a set of retained node ids. Initially all node ids are retained. removed ID list.\n\n- Sort nodes by depth descending, then id. For each node in that order:\n   - if node id not in retained set -> skip (already removed? actually we shouldn't encounter removed nodes because we only update retained set as we go, and sorted list includes all nodes. So we need to check if it's already removed? The node might have been removed as a child? But we only remove node when we process it, we don't remove children when removing parent? Wait, we never remove a node that has retained children. So if we remove a leaf, its parent still has that child? No, when we remove a leaf, we remove the leaf node itself. The parent still might have other children. The parent is not directly affected. In subsequent processing of parent, we check its children: if any child is retained (i.e., not removed), we cannot remove parent. So after removing a leaf, the parent still has that child removed, so the child set changes. So we need to dynamically check children retained status. So when processing node, we need to look at its current children (ids from children dict) and see if any of those ids are in retained set. So we need to maintain retained set.\n\n   - But note: If we remove a node, its children are not automatically removed from the tree? In a typical deletion of a subtree, if you delete a parent, you delete its descendants too. But here the rule about \"retained children\" suggests that you can't delete a node if its children are still there. That implies that if you want to delete a parent, you must first delete all its children. So children are independent; they can be deleted separately. So removal of a node does not cause removal of its descendants. The children remain as nodes in the tree, but without a parent? That's odd. Actually, the tree is defined by parent pointers. If you remove a node, what happens to its children? They become disconnected. Normally in a tree structure, if you remove an interior node, you must either remove its subtree or reattach children to grandparent. The problem statement mentions \"replacement candidates\" so maybe there is replacement. However, the given constraints: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" suggests that you cannot remove a node if any of its children remain because that would leave them orphaned. So the valid operation is: either remove a leaf node, or remove a node after all its children have been removed (effectively removing the entire subtree). So when you remove a leaf, its parent still has that child removed. So the number of children of the parent decreases. Eventually, if all children of a parent are removed, the parent becomes a leaf and can be removed if other conditions met.\n\n   - So algorithm: process nodes in depth order. For each node, check if removable, symbol not required, and has no retained children (i.e., for each child id, child not in retained set). Then check required symbols condition. If all pass, remove it (add to removed list, remove from retained set). Note: if a node has no children initially, it qualifies.\n\n   - Important: When we remove a node, its parent might later become removable. That's fine because we process parent after children (since parent has lower depth). So we don't need to revisit nodes.\n\n   - However, the order of processing by descending depth ensures that leaves are processed first. But what about nodes at same depth? Tie break by ID. That's fine.\n\n   - One more thing: The required symbols condition: after removal of this node, all required symbols must still be present in retained set. So we need to check if the node's symbol is required? Actually, if node's symbol is required, we already skip removal (by \"Never remove a required symbol\"). So we only consider nodes with symbol not required. But we still need to ensure that removing this node does not cause loss of a required symbol that is only present in this node. Since its symbol is not required, it won't directly affect required symbols count. However, if the node has children that might be the only ones with required symbols, but those children are leaves? For example, a node that is not required but its subtree contains the only occurrence of a required symbol. If we remove this node, we would also remove? Wait, we are not removing children; we only remove this node itself. The children remain! So removing a node does not remove its children. So the required symbols in descendants are unaffected. The only potential loss is if this node itself has a required symbol. But we prevent removing nodes with required symbols. So actually, for nodes with non-required symbols, removing them won't affect required symbols count at all, because the symbol counts for required symbols only come from nodes with that symbol. So we don't need to check required symbols for non-required nodes? Wait: Consider a node whose symbol is not required, but it is the parent of the only node with a required symbol. If we remove this parent, the child remains, so required symbol is still present. So no issue. So the required symbols check is only relevant for nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols. But we already prohibit removing those. Therefore, the check \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" might be redundant given the \"never remove a required symbol\" rule. But maybe there is a case where a node with a required symbol could be removed if there is another node with same symbol? The \"never remove\" says absolute - never remove a required symbol. So it's stricter. So we can skip the required symbols count check entirely because it will never be violated for nodes we consider (since we only consider nodes with symbol not required). However, there might be a subtlety: If a node is not a required symbol but its children are the only ones with a required symbol and we remove the parent? But we don't remove children; they still remain. So fine.\n\n   - But check the description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That is a condition. Maybe it's intended to be applied even for non-required nodes because removing a node might also remove the subtree? But the rule \"never remove node with retained children\" ensures children stay. So no. Perhaps the intended model is that removing a node removes its entire subtree. Let's examine the fixtures: In a-000, node n2 has children n4. n2 is removable, n4 is removable. If we remove n2, do we also remove n4? If we remove n2 alone, n4 remains but becomes a child of nothing? That would be invalid. The rule \"never remove a node with retained children\" would prevent removing n2 as long as n4 is retained. So to remove n2, we must first remove n4. So the process would be: remove n4 first (if conditions allow), then remove n2. That matches typical subtree deletion. So yes, removal is per-node but children must be already removed. So the subtree is removed node by node from leaves upwards.\n\n   - So the required symbols condition: When we remove a leaf node with a non-required symbol, it doesn't affect required symbols. When we remove an interior node after all its children are removed, it also doesn't affect required symbols because the children are already gone. So the condition only matters for nodes whose symbol is required? But we don't remove those. So maybe it's a safety check. We'll implement it anyway: before removing, compute counts of required symbols in retained set, check if removing this node would cause any to drop to zero. Since node symbol is not required, it won't affect. So it's trivially satisfied. But we'll implement it for completeness.\n\n   - However, what if a node has a required symbol but removable? In fixtures, n4 has symbol s1 which is required, but removable=true. According to \"never remove a required symbol\", we should not remove n4. So the algorithm should not remove n4. That's consistent with rule.\n\n   - So algorithm:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])  # set of strings\n    # Build structures\n    node_map = {}\n    children_map = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        node_map[nid] = n\n        children_map[nid] = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            children_map[pid].append(n['id'])\n    # Initialize retained set with all ids\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    removed = []\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id (string)\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        # Check if already removed (unlikely but skip)\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        # Conditions to remove:\n        # 1. removable\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        # 2. symbol not required\n        if n['symbol'] in required:\n            continue\n        # 3. no retained children (all children already removed)\n        children = children_map[nid]\n        has_retained_child = any(child in retained for child in children)\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # 4. check required symbols still present after removal\n        # (since symbol not required, we just check that for each req symbol, there exists at least one node with that symbol in retained - {nid})\n        # But we need to compute counts. We'll compute counts of each required symbol in retained set. Then decrement for this node's symbol.\n        sym_counts = {}\n        for rid in retained:\n            s = node_map[rid]['symbol']\n            if s in required:\n                sym_counts[s] = sym_counts.get(s, 0)+1\n        # Now simulate removal\n        if n['symbol'] in required:\n            # should not happen due to condition 2, but defensive\n            continue\n        # Since n's symbol not required, no change in required counts\n        # Actually, we still need to ensure that all required symbols are present. Already present.\n        # So proceed\n        # Remove node\n        removed.append(nid)\n        retained.remove(nid)\n    # After loop, retained_ids are the ids left\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)  # sort alphabetically\n    # removed order is as we removed\n    result = {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids\n    }\n    return result\n\nBut need to verify with fixtures. Let's simulate for case a-000:\nnodes: root (depth0, not removable, required symbol root), n1 (depth1, symbol s1, not removable, required), n2 (depth1, symbol s2, removable, not required), n3 (depth1, symbol s0, not removable, not required), n4 (depth2, symbol s1, removable, required), n5 (depth2, symbol s2, not removable, not required), n6 (depth2, symbol s0, removable, not required).\nrequired = {\"root\",\"s1\"}.\nSorted by depth descending: depths: n4(2), n5(2), n6(2), then n1(1), n2(1), n3(1), root(0). At equal depth, tie break by id: n4, n5, n6 (since n4 < n5 < n6 lexicographically). At depth1: n1, n2, n3.\nProcess n4: removable? yes. symbol s1 is required -> skip because condition 2 (symbol in required). So not removed.\nProcess n5: removable? no (false) -> skip.\nProcess n6: removable? yes, symbol s0 not required, no retained children? n6 has no children (none in children_map). So condition passes. No required symbol issue. Remove n6: removed=[n6], retained loses n6.\nProcess n1: removable? false -> skip.\nProcess n2: removable? yes, symbol s2 not required, has retained children? n2's children: n4. n4 is still retained (not removed). So has_retained_child true -> skip.\nProcess n3: removable? false -> skip.\nProcess root: removable? false -> skip.\nResult: removed = ['n6']; retained = all others. retained sorted: ['n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root']? Actually root id 'root', others n1..n5. So retained_ids = ['n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root'].\nCheck if that matches expected? Not given, but plausible.\n\nWhat about case a-001: similar, but n4 has symbol s1 (required) and removable, n6 has s0 removable. n5 not removable. So same result: only n6 removed.\n\nCase a-002: similar.\n\nSo algorithm seems plausible.\n\nEdge: If there were multiple nodes with same required symbol, we could remove one if it's removable? But rule \"never remove a required symbol\" prevents that. So consistent.\n\nNow we need to implement solve(case) accordingly.\n\nAlso need to ensure we handle the case where a node's parent is None (root). We already have that.\n\nOutput must be JSON serializable. We'll return dict with lists.\n\nNow produce implementation as Python source string inside a JSON object with key \"implementation\". Escape quotes, newlines.\n\nLet's write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2054,"completion_tokens":5661,"total_tokens":7715,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5277},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":6},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}